WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:02.732
STEPHANIE WONG: One of the
linchpins of on-prem systems

00:00:02.732 --> 00:00:04.580
is the aggressive
use of IP addresses

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.590
to balance, scatter,
and manage traffic

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.820
coming into your environment.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.378
This is built into the
configuration of your routers,

00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:13.670
firewalls, and most of the
hardware and software you've

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:14.605
got lying around.

00:00:14.605 --> 00:00:16.980
As you migrate portions of
your environment to the cloud,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.060
however, you have to be aware
that this IP address logic will

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:21.950
change in various ways.

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:24.620
As such, it's crucial to plan
ahead for your IP address

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.720
changes that could
affect your workloads

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.390
and how they interact with
internal and public network

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:30.800
traffic.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.220
In this episode, I'll be
covering private and public IPs

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.950
on Google Cloud, and how
you can achieve remappable

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.380
IP addresses for
your cloud resources.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:39.890
Stay tuned.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.610
The main challenge is
that you can't simply

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:51.250
move your existing IP
addresses to the cloud

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.020
along with your services.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.540
Cloud providers and data
centers have a limited pool

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.060
of IP addresses that
they own, and they often

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:02.300
reuse previously-assigned IPs
in order to maximize them.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.390
The result is that your
services will receive

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:08.170
a dynamically-assigned internal
and public IP address, which

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.330
by default are
ephemeral, meaning

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:14.190
if you restart those instances,
you'll lose those IPs.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.080
For companies in
this situation, there

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.520
are basically two solutions
which you could run through.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.940
Define firewall rules by using
tags instead of IP addresses.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.710
Forward traffic
through a managed load

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.610
balancer with a static IP.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.790
Of course, both will
work, but they both

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.690
come with a large downside.

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:35.120
As your cloud IPs
continue to change,

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.730
they'll break any dependencies
your services might

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.640
have on those IPs
which are hardcoded

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.920
or require a network
admin to manually change,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.520
which can quickly turn into
a nightmare of maintenance.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.020
For example, let's say you spin
up your first back-end machines

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.080
in the cloud and decide to tear
them down for a period of time

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.730
to save on costs.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.310
Suddenly, when you're ready to
get them up and running again,

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:01.340
you'll notice that you lost
those dynamically-assigned IPs,

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.870
only to find new ones.

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:04.940
Now you have to reconfigure
your on-prem app

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.610
and any firewall rules to
point to the new public IP

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.139
of your cloud back-end.

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.660
This can grow pretty
tiresome and unwieldy

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:13.070
over large environments.

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:15.020
Thankfully, Google
Cloud allows you

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.150
to get around this
situation by reserving

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.890
static IPs for your services.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.010
So if your VM is
shut down, you can

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.290
retain the same
internal and public IPs

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:25.520
when you spin it back up.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.830
What this means is that
you can feel relaxed

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:30.470
knowing that if you point
a forwarding rule, firewall

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:32.600
rule, or application
to your cloud instance

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.870
IP from within or out
of the VPC network,

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.060
you won't need to
reconfigure the IPs each time

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.805
you shut down your instances.

00:02:39.805 --> 00:02:41.180
This really comes
in handy if you

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:43.925
are dependent on a specific
IP address for your service

00:02:43.925 --> 00:02:45.800
and need to prevent your
teammates from being

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.600
able to use that address.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:48.950
And the best part?

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:51.530
You can even promote a
previously ephemeral IP

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:56.070
to be a static one, helping you
cut corners in a few places.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.210
Let's dive into IPs a bit more.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:01.250
For Compute Engine, you can
assign one internal and one

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.088
public IP address for each VM.

00:03:04.088 --> 00:03:05.630
It's important to
note that you don't

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.660
need public IPs for your
VMs within the same VPC

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.760
to communicate,
and this can really

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.020
help network performance.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.590
On the other hand, if you
need the VM to communicate

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.070
with the internet or any VM
in a different VPC network,

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.470
your VM has to have
a public IP address,

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.080
unless you've set up
some sort of proxy or VPN

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.060
in the other network.

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.940
For both cases of internal
and public IP addresses,

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:31.680
you can choose ephemeral
and static IPs.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.560
Let's start with internal IPs.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:36.830
When you create a VM, unless
you specify an internal IP

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:39.080
within your subnet
range, Google will just

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.540
assign your VM an
ephemeral internal IP

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.340
from your subnet range.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.800
You can reserve one later
on and assign it to your VM

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.140
if you decide you need a
static internal IP later.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.570
For public IPs,
the same applies.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:53.690
Unless you assign your VM a
reserved static public IP,

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:57.230
Google will assign an ephemeral
public IP randomly from a pool,

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.380
or you can choose not to
assign a public IP whatsoever,

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.360
which can be good
for keeping instances

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.460
from being exposed to
the public internet

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.350
and reduce security risk.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.780
Later on, you can
always assign one.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:12.650
There's also a way to create
a custom public IP on GCP, as

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.670
well, but I'll point you to
the documentation linked below.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.640
Let's walk through IP setup.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:23.090
Let's take a look at a VM I just
created with Apache 2 installed

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:25.490
deployed in useast-1.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:28.420
First, head to the
Networking tab.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.150
By default, you're given an
ephemeral internal and public

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:32.350
address.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.150
I'm going to remove the
ephemeral public IP,

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.100
and you can now see it
has no public IP address.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:41.800
It's important to note that if
you're an internal VM talking

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.290
to other internal
VMs, then you need

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:48.110
to be using the internal address
for the best performance.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:50.590
Otherwise, your internal
VM has to travel back

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.890
to the public internet,
which can slow it down.

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:56.260
Now, as I mentioned,
by default, your VMs

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:59.240
will use ephemeral public
IPs, but sometimes you

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.840
need static ones that
won't change over time.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.530
Thankfully, you can create
this right in Google Cloud.

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:09.550
Go to the Network tab, and
then in External IP Addresses,

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:11.210
create a static one.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.470
And note that we can also
just promote an ephemeral one.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:17.680
You want to create the IP in
the same region as the instance,

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.360
unless you're doing
global forwarding,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.210
because static IPs are
regional resources.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:25.810
You can assign the IP to the
instance right from here.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.810
And now you see we have a
new static public IP address

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:30.510
assigned to the instance.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.260
And as long as you
have the right firewall

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:35.840
setup to allow HTTP
traffic on port 80,

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.380
now you can see that we
have access to the web page

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.960
through the new
static IP address.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:44.170
Once you understand how
IP addresses are affected

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:45.700
when different
actions are performed

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:47.860
in your overall
architecture, you

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:50.530
start to realize the importance
of using remappable IP

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:53.428
addresses, especially for
front-end servers in the cloud.

00:05:53.428 --> 00:05:55.720
But what if you don't want
a public IP address in order

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.510
to maintain higher
security, yet you still

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:00.760
want to be able to do
things like fetch updates

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.500
from the public internet?

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.960
Stay tuned for the next
episode to find out how.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.390
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:09.010
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:12.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

